FINDINGS:

1. [BLOCKER] src/runtime.rs:509 — `Duration::from_secs_f64(seconds)` panics for any finite `seconds` that overflows `Duration` (e.g. `rb_set_clock(1e300)` or `f64::MAX`); the guard at line 508 only rejects non-finite/negative values, and the comment directly below at lines 530–532 claims such a host value "gets a clean error, not an abort". This is reachable from the exported host ABI (`rb_set_clock`, src/wasm.rs:920) and aborts the wasm module or unwinds out of the interpreter rather than producing the documented timestamp-less record. Fix: use `Duration::try_from_secs_f64(seconds).ok()` (treat overflow as "no clock"). — VERIFIED: traced `rb_set_clock` (src/wasm.rs:920-922) → `set_host_clock` (src/runtime.rs:506-513); line 508 admits `1e300`, line 509 calls the panicking constructor. (Could not run the wasm target in this session; the trace is exact.)

2. [MAJOR] src/logging.rs:126 — `chrono::DateTime::<chrono::Utc>::from(instant)` is not fallible: chrono's `From<SystemTime>` unwraps `timestamp_opt`, which panics for instants outside its range. `runtime::current_instant()`/`now()` only rejects values that overflow `SystemTime` (`checked_add`, src/runtime.rs:533), so a host clock such as `rb_set_clock(1e16)` is accepted and then panics inside an enabled logging call instead of writing a record without a timestamp. Fix: use `DateTime::from_timestamp(secs, nanos)`/`timestamp_opt` and fall back to `None` (the same "no clock" path) on `None`. Noticed but not run; a wasm-target test with a large finite clock would settle it.

3. [MAJOR] src/logging.rs:124 — the newly documented no-clock fallback (`current_instant().ok()?` → `None`, SPEC.md:1264-1268) has no test. All timestamp tests use `set_timestamps(no)` or a working native clock; the `None`-because-no-clock branch is unreachable natively because `rb_set_clock` is a no-op on non-wasm targets (src/wasm.rs:923-928). This is a new branch with no test on either side. Add a wasm-target test or an injectable clock seam in `format_timestamp`.

4. [MAJOR] src/runtime.rs:3566-3571 — a logging write failure is constructed as `Error::Io(format!(...))` with no source span, while every argument/level error in the same module is `Error::Runtime(..., span)`. A caught or uncaught `IoError` therefore cannot point at the `logging.info` line, only at the builtin name. Fix: carry the call's `span` into the logging failure (or append the position) so the error is locatable like its siblings.

5. [MINOR] tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt:27-29 — the header counts are stale: it claims "SPEC.md 42" and "60 of 111 blocks", but `SPEC.md:1231` was appended at line 98, giving 43 SPEC rows and 61 total RUNS. The new entry is also misplaced under the `# --- HANDOUT.md` section (lines 84-98) instead of the SPEC section. Fix the counts and move the entry next to the other `SPEC.md:` rows.

